    [BugFix] Preserve GLM-Next shared-pool KV budget (#19457)
    
    ### What this PR does / why we need it?
    
    GLM-5.3-Flash MRV1 fails the 1M-context KV capacity check after #19079
    correctly classifies KPool attention as SFA. This sets
    `use_sparse=True`, bypasses the generic hybrid-backing exemption, and
    triggers a per-layer discount even though the GLM-Next planner already
    accounts for physical sharing. The failing nightly applies a factor of
    `30916608 / 112386048 = 0.2751`, leaving about 6.23-6.39 GiB for
    admission instead of the 7.57 GiB required.
    
    Extend the existing DeepSeek V4/V4.1 shared-pool exemption to cache
    specs marked `model_version="glm5_next"`, using the same implementation
    approach as #19494. Both model runners consume the GLM-Next
    physical-slot planner, so this exemption belongs to the shared-pool
    layout rather than a runner-specific branch. The current default MRV2
    hybrid path already preserves its budget through the later generic
    exemption.
    
    Keep SFA classification, cache geometry, deployment YAMLs, and all
    pre-existing test functions intact. Add one parameterized regression
    covering BF16/packed int8 main caches and sparse routing
    enabled/disabled, with exactly the physical budget needed for a
    1,048,576-token context.
    
    Related failure: [Nightly-A3 GLM
    job](https://github.com/vllm-project/vllm-ascend/actions/runs/39632677145/job/119582689564).
    
    ### Does this PR introduce _any_ user-facing change?
    
    Yes. GLM-Next retains its physical shared-pool KV budget instead of
    receiving an unnecessary per-layer reduction, addressing the
    GLM-5.3-Flash MRV1 startup capacity failure without reducing
    `max_model_len` or changing deployment configuration.
    
    ### How was this patch tested?
    
    **CPU regression tests in the reproduced nightly image, without NPU
    devices:**
    
    ```bash
    TORCH_DEVICE_BACKEND_AUTOLOAD=0 python3 -m pytest -q -p no:cacheprovider \
      tests/ut/models/test_glm5next_cache_config.py \
      tests/ut/worker/test_worker_v1.py \
      -k 'glm or budget_scaling or shared_tuple_layout or multi_group_budget'
    ```
    
    - Current implementation: **13 passed**, including all four new BF16/C8
    x sparse-on/off cases and the existing GLM, generic scaling, and
    DeepSeek shared-pool tests.
    - Unmodified main-base worker with the four new cases: **2 failed, 2
    passed**. Both sparse cases reproduce the erroneous budget reduction;
    both non-sparse controls pass.
    - The new cases assert the complete planner budget, 1M physical
    capacity, and the sum of physical allocation descriptor sizes. All
    pre-existing test functions and `tests/ut/worker/test_worker_v1.py`
    remain unchanged.
    - Ruff check/format check, Python source compilation, and `git diff
    --check` pass. `bash format.sh ci` stops because `pre-commit` is
    unavailable locally; full hooks remain for CI.
    
    **Earlier A3 decoder evidence:**
    
    The original nightly MRV1 failed the 7.57 GiB admission check. The
    earlier MRV1-only implementation of this PR passed all eight decoder
    health checks in 445.24 s with 22.74 GiB of KV budget. Unmodified MRV2
    passed all eight in 450.25 s with 23.32-23.33 GiB. These runs used the
    same nightly image and GLM weights, DP8/TP2, 1M context, int8 KV, MTP5,
    and decode graph capture.
    
    Those hardware results establish the diagnosis and the earlier
    implementation; they are not a hardware rerun of the current shared
    exemption. NPU startup with the final implementation, full PD requests,
    accuracy, and throughput/latency validation remain pending before merge.
    
    ---
    
